<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles the Good</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1084–<a href="/years/1127">1127</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Charles I, Count of Flanders</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles the Good was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1119 until 1127. A son of King Canute IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and Adela of Flanders, he was raised at the Flemish court and succeeded his cousin Baldwin VII. Known for his piety, for keeping the peace and for relieving famine, he was murdered in the church of St Donatian at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> on 2 March 1127 by members of the Erembald clan, setting off a succession crisis recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galbert-of-bruges">Galbert of Bruges</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-good.jpg" alt="Charles the Good" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sint-Salvatorskathedraal_-_Bruges_-_IMG_4774.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
